\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Economists Say Sol to Continue Strengthening Trend\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2012\par 18:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Peru's sol strengthens more than 3% against U.S. dollar so far this year\par \par \uc2 --Economists see more room for appreciation\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ryan Dube \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LIMA--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's sol should maintain its trend of strengthening against the U.S. dollar over the next few years, though short-term volatility in the exchange rate should be expected, economists say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Peruvian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been gaining against the dollar during the last several years amid strong economic growth in the Andean country. The sol has appreciated more than 3% against the dollar so far this year, while in 2011 it strengthened 3.92%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Peru's economy has been driven by private-sector investments in recent years. In 2011, Peru's gross domestic product gained 6.9%, while in 2010 it rose 8.8%. In the first half of this year, Peru's economy expanded 6.11%. Economic growth for the full-year 2012 is expected to be about 6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chief economist of \f28 \uc2 \f2 BBVA\f28 \uc2 \f2  Banco Continental, Hugo Perea, said during an interview the trend for the sol hasn't changed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the medium term, I think that there is still space for the sol to strengthen," Mr. Perea said. "The fundamentals continue to be very strong for Peru."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BBVA\f28 \uc2 \f2  Banco Continental (CONTINC1.VL), Peru's second biggest bank, expects the sol to finish 2012 at PEN2.60 per dollar, which would be an appreciation of about 3.6% for the year. By 2015, the bank forecasts that the sol will reach PEN2.50 per dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Perea said the forecasts consider that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Central Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to ease volatility. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has bought about $9 billion this year in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, closing in on its 2007 record of $10.3 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to continue buying dollars while the sol appreciates, Mr. Perea said. "While the economy keeps growing, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  without creating inflationary pressures," Mr. Perea said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pedro Pablo Kuczynski, a well-known economist who has served as finance minister and prime minister, said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are necessary to protect local industry that would be hurt by a rapid appreciation of the sol. The sol has been one of Latin America's least volatile \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  thanks to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We would be swept away if we let the exchange rate move with the natural market," said Mr. Kuczynski. "I think they should \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alonso Seguro, the chief economist of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco de Credito del Peru\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the country's largest bank, said that only a major "shock" would reverse the sol's trend of appreciating in the medium-term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the short-term, however, volatility should be expected as uncertainty in the global economy continues, the economists say. In May, investor concerns surrounding the euro zone debt crisis resulted in the sol temporarily depreciating against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are likely going to see some more hiccups that are going to be difficult to anticipate," Mr. Seguro said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banco de Credito\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CREDITC1.VL) forecasts that the sol will finish 2012 at PEN2.60 per dollar, and to end 2013 at PEN2.55.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said that Peru's closely managed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "still stands as one of the most overvalued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It added that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  recent upgrade of Peru's debt rating will help the appreciation trend to continue. "The observed behavior during August, particularly after the rating upgrade, will only accentuate this trend," it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Ryan Dube at ryan.dube@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribe to WSJ: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2 \f2  [ 20-08-12 1642GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020120820e88k0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
