\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Genco Catches Big Wave From Debt Restructuring\b0\par\par\uc2 1,255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2012\par 19:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:59 EDT - Investors are applauding Genco's (GNK) debt-restructuring deal that defers all 2013 principal payments in exchange for a $99.9M prepayment and increased funding costs on its largest credit facility. That as the dry-bulk-shipping market a "favorable" sector outlook for the first time in 5 years, says Jefferies. With debt deal "essentially eliminating all significant cash commitments for the company until" April Fool's 2014, the investment bank contends GNK "has created ample runway...to benefit from the eventual turnaround in the dry bulk shipping market in either 2H or 2013 without diluting current equity holders." Shares jump 39% to $2.90, though GNK is down 57% for the year. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:48 EDT - Kellogg (K) CEO John Bryant calls for EPA to temporary waive a requirement that a certain percentage of the corn crop be used in ethanol production. "To continue to have 40% of the U.S. corn crop going into ethanol production is very questionable policy," Bryant said in an interview with Dow Jones. He joins a host of US lawmakers and industry groups calling for a cut in the mandate. Corn prices soared to historic highs last month as the worst Midwest drought in decades cut US crop yields across the heart of the corn belt. (andrew.johnsonjr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:10 EDT - While it might only be a "modest" beat in Dahlman Rose's words, investors aren't treating \uc2 Diana Shipping\uc2 's (DSX) shares as such as the beaten-down stock gets a lift from the company's 2Q report. DSX has reached several new time-charter deals and has fixed 56% of its 2013 days already. "This compares very favorably" to peers, notes Dahlman. It adds with shares still trading far below net-asset value, share repurchases under DSX's $100M buyback effort "will help bridge the valuation gap." Shares are up 4.7% to $6.86, cutting the past year's drop to 26%. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:03 EDT - Investors are applauding Gentiva's (GTIV) sizeable 2Q EPS beat and ignoring the revenue miss as "cost-cutting efforts continue to aid home-health performance, with hospice contribution margins starting to improve off of the disappointing 1Q," notes Raymond James. It adds GTIV's forecast reiteration "looks somewhat conservative now given the strong 2Q performance amidst healthy cost-control initiatives. Overall, 2Q was certainly a step in the right direction...although we continue to have concerns with lack of top-line growth and upcoming reimbursement changes for home health and hospice (including the sequestration impact)." GTIV gains 7% to $6.99, pushing the stock into the green for 2012. It's down 49%, though, from a year earlier. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:58 EDT - Despite an uncertain economic recovery and a stronger US dollar, "first and foremost the fundamentals of our business, customer interest and market demand remain intact," Ansys (ANSS) CEO James Cashman told analysts on today's earnings call. The maker of engineering and simulation software is enjoying consistent demand from companies in automotive, aerospace, defense and material and chemical processing industries, which made 2Q "really a strong quarter for us" Cashman said. He also reaffirmed full-year revenue and earnings expectations. ANSS up 9.4% at $65.56. (steve-d.jones@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:52 EDT - Knight Capital (KCG) said Wednesday's electronic-trading glitches resulted in a trading loss of about $440M, but the market seems to think that's not nearly enough. KCG has slumped 70% the past 2 days, including 56% today, representing a market-share loss of about $712M since Tuesday's close. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:51 EDT - U.S. stocks bouncing off fresh session lows as investors come to grips with a letdown from ECB's Draghi this morning. ECB's decision to essentially take no substantive action right now sent a surging euro into a free-fall and shoved Spanish 10-yr bond yields scurrying back above 7%. Meanwhile, US dollar strengthens, Treasurys rally and stocks slump, led by energy, materials and financials. DJIA down 151, earlier down as much as 192. Nasdaq falls 21, S&P 500 off 17. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:50 EDT - There are no signs that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  will change its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease the short term volatility of the sol against the USD, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  says. "We expect the PEN to continue its slow strengthening trend on the back of foreign inflows into the mining sector, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moderating short-term volatility," the bank says. So far this year, \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought $8.5B to slow the appreciation of the sol. The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated about 2.6% against the greenback this year. (ryan.dube@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:42 EDT - Some are scratching their heads about Apollo's (APO) stock slump today since the private-equity firm's 2Q results handily beat expectations. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  calls the results "healthy" as APO showed "much higher fee-related earnings and better-than-expected performance fees." And amid the company's 24c/share distribution, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says APO's "strong cash earnings generation/dividend payout remains an underappreciated feature." Shares are down 5.4% at $13.25, nearly halving the 2012 increase they had seen through Wednesday. (ryan.dezember@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:39 EDT - Green Mountain (GMCR), whose stock has taken a beating since its nearly $116 September high, climbs 32% to $23.64 following company's detailing of a plan to better forecast sales. After struggling this year with excess inventory hurting its profitability, GMCR said it made finding a better forecasting method "its highest priority....And we found a better way of doing it that gives us a much better handle on consumer demand going forward," CEO Larry Blanford said in an interview. (annie.gasparro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:35 EDT - The \uc2 Dow\uc2  industrials has now given back more than half of what it gained during last week's rally. Down 184 at 12787, has retraced some 55% of the gain off the July 24 intraday low of 12521.84 to Monday's high of 13128.64. The 50% mark is 12825.24. The key 61.8% Fibonacci retracement level at 12753.64 is the next target. Falling below that would target a full retracement. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:29 EDT - \uc2 Moody's\uc2  flags Latin American exporters and commodities producers as vulnerable to a "protracted recession" in Europe. Companies that limit most of their trade to Latin America are comparatively sheltered. "The recession in Europe would exacerbate the slowdown in China," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. "That's because China is both a major exporter to the EU and a significant importer from Latin America, which would face a reduction in demand from both markets." Latin American firms with the most revenue exposure to Europe include Brazil's \uc2 Fibria Celulose\uc2  (FIBR3.BR) and \uc2 Magnesita Refratarios\uc2  (MAGG3.BR) and Peru's Camposol (CSOL.OS), \uc2 Moody's\uc2  notes. (paul.kiernan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-02-12 1359ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120802e882000pq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
