\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: No Sign  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Will Change  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Policy\b0\par\par\uc2 1,407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2012\par 18:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:50 EDT - There are no signs that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  will change its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease the short term volatility of the sol against the USD, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  says. "We expect the PEN to continue its slow strengthening trend on the back of foreign inflows into the mining sector, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moderating short-term volatility," the bank says. So far this year, \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought $8.5B to slow the appreciation of the sol. The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated about 2.6% against the greenback this year. (ryan.dube@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:42 EDT - Some are scratching their heads about Apollo's (APO) stock slump today since the private-equity firm's 2Q results handily beat expectations. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  calls the results "healthy" as APO showed "much higher fee-related earnings and better-than-expected performance fees." And amid the company's 24c/share distribution, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says APO's "strong cash earnings generation/dividend payout remains an underappreciated feature." Shares are down 5.4% at $13.25, nearly halving the 2012 increase they had seen through Wednesday. (ryan.dezember@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:39 EDT - Green Mountain (GMCR), whose stock has taken a beating since its nearly $116 September high, climbs 32% to $23.64 following company's detailing of a plan to better forecast sales. After struggling this year with excess inventory hurting its profitability, GMCR said it made finding a better forecasting method "its highest priority....And we found a better way of doing it that gives us a much better handle on consumer demand going forward," CEO Larry Blanford said in an interview. (annie.gasparro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:35 EDT - The \uc2 Dow\uc2  industrials has now given back more than half of what it gained during last week's rally. Down 184 at 12787, has retraced some 55% of the gain off the July 24 intraday low of 12521.84 to Monday's high of 13128.64. The 50% mark is 12825.24. The key 61.8% Fibonacci retracement level at 12753.64 is the next target. Falling below that would target a full retracement. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:29 EDT - \uc2 Moody's\uc2  flags Latin American exporters and commodities producers as vulnerable to a "protracted recession" in Europe. Companies that limit most of their trade to Latin America are comparatively sheltered. "The recession in Europe would exacerbate the slowdown in China," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. "That's because China is both a major exporter to the EU and a significant importer from Latin America, which would face a reduction in demand from both markets." Latin American firms with the most revenue exposure to Europe include Brazil's \uc2 Fibria Celulose\uc2  (FIBR3.BR) and \uc2 Magnesita Refratarios\uc2  (MAGG3.BR) and Peru's Camposol (CSOL.OS), \uc2 Moody's\uc2  notes. (paul.kiernan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:24 EDT - That 955M monthly users \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB) said last week it as of June 30? Chances are the actual number of people is a good bit less. Tucked in the 2Q report filed this week with the SEC is a disclosure that FB estimates 4.8% of its monthly users are likely a duplicate account, with another 2.4% being user-misclassified (created for a business, group or pet) and 1.5% being "undesirable." They "represent user profiles that we determine are intended to be used for purposes that violate our terms of service, such as spamming." FB shares have continued to weaken, and are on the verge of carrying a 1-handle as the stock is down another 4% today to $20.05. They're down 15% this week. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:17 EDT - Here is the question: How can the ECB help bring down long-dated yields in Spain and Italy if a revised bond-buy program purchases only short-dated debt? Dario Perkins, analyst at Lombard Street Research, argues Draghi's plan "sounds even less impressive" than the Securities Market Program, known as SMP. "Apparently, the ECB wants to avoid any hint of debt monetization by stealth," Perkins says. He highlights three key issues: 1.) definitively separating weak banks from their weak sovereigns; 2.) allowing European governments to fund themselves at manageable interest rates; and 3.) restoring economic growth in the region. "Until policymakers take control of all three of these issues, the euro crisis can only escalate from here," he says. "With \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  re-denomination risk still a genuine concern -- despite what the ECB claims -- our advice is to stay well away from all periphery debt." (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:15 EDT - Atlas Air (AAWW) CEO Bill Flynn says he expects a peak shipping season this fall but stops well short of forecasting magnitude. Still, "there was no peak in 2011" for air-cargo carriers, he notes on post-earnings conference call, so any uptick is positive. Flynn says high-tech product launches, as well as low business inventories, should "underpin demand for air-freight" in 2H. On full-year EPS guidance, however, he declines to do more than reiterate previous full-year forecast for "more than" $5.10. "The guidance that we have given shows good earnings growth...and suggests a peak," Flynn says. AAWW up 0.9% to $45.68. bob.sechler@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:52 EDT - \uc2 American Eagle Outfitters\uc2  (AEO) down 2.6% to $19.80, sucked into the vortex of selling surrounding teen retailers as \uc2 Abercrombie & Fitch\uc2  (ANF) and Aeropostale (ARO) warned and others missed July same-store sales expectations. AEO's drop follows company lifting 2Q earnings outlook and garners positive comments from analysts. "Under new leadership -- CEO and executive chairman -- we believe that AEO will further improve inventory productivity, the composition of the store fleet and the reach of the brands through innovation," B. Riley says. "The AE brand is extremely strong and relevant and the enhanced management team is positioning the company to leverage that strength," firm adds. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:49 EDT - Investors hoping to discern if they can rule out a potential \uc2 Dollar Thrifty\uc2  (DTG) bid by \uc2 Avis\uc2  (CAR) didn't get a clear answer from CAR management during the company's earnings call. When asked to "unequivocally rule out participating in further industry consolidation in North America," CAR said its disclosures on DTG were already out there "and we've told people where we stand on it." CAR ended its pursuit of DTG in Sept, though the DTG takeover saga resurfaced this week when it challenged Hertz (HTZ) and others to either make an appealing takeover bid or allow it to move forward as a standalone company. CAR up 4.4% and DTG up 5.6%. (john.kell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:49 EDT - A down day for stocks shows that investors are disappointed by the lack of ECB action, but it's also a down day for the Volatility Index--known as the market's fear gauge. That shows statements that the bank is ready to restart purchases of government bonds were enough to ease investor fears. The VIX, which typically moves inversely to the S&P 500 edges lower to 18.94 even as the index falls 1.2%. (kaitlyn.kiernan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 EDT - The massive selloff in 10-year Spain and Italy government bonds gathers more stream as Spain's Rajoy declines to say whether Spain would seek help via the euro zone's bailout fund. Meanwhile, Monti insists Italy won't need a bailout. Rather than reassuring investors, that creates more stress because Draghi said earlier the ECB won't buy bonds before that country's government requests such assistance. The 10-year Spanish yield just hit a fresh session peak, up nearly a half-point on the day at 7.213% while Italy jumps 0.39 point to 6.327%. In contrast, a flight into Treasurys has sent that market's 10-year yield down 0.08 point to 1.459%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-02-12 1250ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120802e882000nt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
