\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Expects Sol To Strengthen Through 2015\b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2012\par 18:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Peru sees sol strengthening to average PEN2.67 per U.S. dollar this year\par \par \uc2 --Finance Ministry says GDP is expected to expand by 6% in 2012 and 2013\par \par \uc2 --The ministry says it sees a trade surplus of $8.3 billion in 2012\par \par \uc2 --Copper production is expected to rise by almost 75% to 2.15 million tons by 2015\par \par \uc2 By Ryan Dube\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's government expects the sol to strengthen this year to an average of PEN2.67 per U.S. dollar, compared with PEN2.75 in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry said Thursday in its multiannual macroeconomic report that the sol's average exchange rate against the greenback is expected to continue to appreciate from 2013 through 2015.\par \par \uc2 The ministry projects that the sol will average PEN2.64 per dollar in 2013, while in 2014 it is seen at PEN2.62 to the dollar and in 2015 it is forecast to average PEN2.60.\par \par \uc2 In the first four months of 2012, the sol gained more than 2% against the dollar. The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since lost those gains as investors seek a safe haven in the dollar due to the euro-zone crisis.\par \par \uc2 The sudden change in trend has led the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell dollars. On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $282 million, which followed the selling of $115 million on Tuesday and $2 million on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's president, Julio Velarde, has said he expects volatility in the exchange rate to continue ahead of the June 17 election in Greece.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the sol closed at PEN2.706 per dollar, which is a depreciation of 0.33% against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the report, the Finance Ministry also said that it forecasts Peru to maintain solid economic growth over the next few years. The ministry expects the gross domestic product will expand 6% in both 2012 and 2013. Annual economic growth is seen at 6.5% in both 2014 and 2015, the report says. Peru's GDP rose by 6.92% in 2011, and 8.8% in 2012.\par \par \uc2 The ministry said Peru's economic growth would be harmed if there is a global economic crisis similar to the 2008 financial crisis, a slowdown in private-sector investments and if a number of projects, including those in the country's key mining sector, are not completed.\par \par \uc2 Private-sector investments are expected to increase 10% in 2012, down from 11.7% last year. Private-sector investments slowed last year from 22.1% growth in 2010 due to uncertainty surrounding the presidential election and the international economy.\par \par \uc2 The ministry forecasts that private-sector investments will expand by 10% in 2013, 12% in 2014 and 12.5% in 2015.\par \par \uc2 It said that Peru is expected to have a trade surplus of $8.3 billion in 2012, down from $9.3 billion in 2011. Exports are forecast to total $48.9 billion in 2012, while imports are seen at $40.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 The government says that exports are expected to reach $63.5 billion in 2015 mainly due to an increase in copper shipments. Imports in 2015 are seen at $55.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 Peru is the world's second-biggest copper producer, and the red metal is the country's main export. In 2011, the country produced 1.24 million tons of copper.\par \par \uc2 Copper production in Peru is expected to rise by almost 75% by 2015 thanks to the start-up of new mines and the completion of expansion projects. The ministry sees Peru's copper production totaling 1.33 million tons this year, 1.45 million tons in 2013, 1.69 million tons in 2014 and 2.15 million tons in 2015.\par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry also said that it expects Peru to have an average fiscal surplus of 1.2% of GDP between 2012 and 2015, compared with 1.3% from 2006 to 2011.\par \par \uc2 -By Ryan Dube, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-945 043 802; peru@dowjones.com [ 05-31-12 1259ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120531e85v000ht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
