\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Could Sell US Dollars -\uc2 JPMorgan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,143 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2012\par 15:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:19 (Dow Jones) \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy about $8.0B this year, could sell greenbacks, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more recently has added dollar liquidity using other instruments. "Overall, the latest BCRP \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaffirms the long-standing view that authorities are more concerned about dampening \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility in what is a de facto crawling peg regime, and they smooth both PEN appreciation and depreciation -- the latter in the context of stubbornly high inflation seen so far this year. Indeed, if external pressures intensify, we would expect the BCRP to sell some of the $8.2 billion it acquired in the first part of this year." (robert.kozak@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:17 (Dow Jones) US April durable goods orders are mildly disappointing, with non-transportation orders down by 0.6% on the month, says Capital Economics. "The massive 21.5% m/m slump in defence orders illustrates just how big a drag the Federal government already is on the economy, even before we get to the fiscal cliff at the start of next year." (jeffrey.sparshott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:11 (Dow Jones) After a brief spike in euro-Swiss franc, the cross is coming back down toward its CHF1.2010 level that it's been sitting near in recent days. Euro back to CHF1.2024 after jumping up to 1.2052 momentarily early in New York trading. Euro hadn't traded above CHF1.2050 since March 30. Traders say the move was not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 . (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:08 (Dow Jones) Traders say they don't see the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  behind this morning's pop in the EUR/CHF. It seemed to be stops being triggered. The euro rose to CHF1.2052. (matt.walter@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:08 (Dow Jones) Brazil's labor market remains tight despite as Latin America's largest economy continues to generate jobs despite slowing activity, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. April's unemployment rate retreated to 6.0% from 6.2% in March. Unemployment at near-record lows "remains a source of cost-push pressure on inflation," firm says. Real wage growth has also been accelerating -- thanks to this year's double-digit hike in Brazil's minimum wage -- which will likely "continue to support private consumption growth," Goldman says. Average monthly wages up 6.2% year-on-year in April to BRL1719.50, although down 1.2% from March, data show. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:03 (Dow Jones) RBC (RY) posted surprisingly higher-than-expected F2Q provisions for credit losses. The reason: two corporate accounts -- a western Canadian real estate project that's been struggling for awhile, and a fraud, says Chief Risk Officer Morten Friis on an earnings call. "Losses are losses but the point is there's nothing systematic about this," he says. RY also had higher PCLs in its C$7B loan book in the Caribbean, which will take another one or two quarters to work through. "Outside of the Caribbean, the portfolio is strong, sound, entirely consistent with our expectations, and I think very encouraging," he says. (caroline.vanhasselt@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:59 (Dow Jones) H-P (HPQ) may be progressing in many areas, but it still hasn't reached that corner it needs to turn, Needham says as it stays bearish. HPQ still has "battles on multiple fronts," all while facing a potentially weakening macro picture. Plan to cut 27k jobs is logical, but Needham calls the restructuring more of a reallocation of resources that will take time to have desired effect, given plan to reinvest most of expected savings into new efforts like cloud. Shares, up 8.5% at $22.88 premarket, erase some of the 32% lost in last year through yesterday's close. (joan.solsman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:58 (Dow Jones) Chilean peso trades slightly stronger against the dollar on a more positive external outlook with rising international copper prices and better-than-expected US durable goods orders data. As Chile produces one third of the world's copper, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often moves in line with that metal's price, while it also looks at US news for direction. "The world looks better today after yesterday's market losses," said Gloria Soto, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at FXCM Chile. Chilean peso recently trading at CLP506.70 versus Wednesday's four-month low close at CLP507.95, according to Valor Futuro newswire. (graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:54 (Dow Jones) US dollar briefly dipped but rebounded quickly after weekly jobless claims were little changed, and durable goods orders in April were better than expected. USD was at Y79.43 from Y79.42 before the goods report, according to EBS via CQG. Euro also steady at $1.2570 from $1.2563. The US indicators unlikely to be a driver today, with markets still sensitive to developments in Europe. (matt.walter@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:50 (Dow Jones) Don't expect morning data to settle debate on where US economy is headed this summer. Good news: jobless claims keep declining and top-line durable goods posted small gain instead of expected small decline. Bad news: demand for non-defense non-aircraft capital goods tumbled 1.9% last month, hinting businesses are less confident about future. Next up: Kansas City Fed manufacturing report at 11:00am EDT. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:49 (Dow Jones) NetApp's (NTAP) bleak F1Q outlook jolted investors late yesterday, while lack of FY 2013 guidance left analysts miffed. "Demand appears even less firm than it did this time last year," notes Sterne Agee, as the company says there's too much uncertainty in Europe to predict beyond this quarter. NTAP insists macro and cyclical factors, not competitive pressure, are to blame for its weak 1Q view, though Janney calls that claim "debatable," given executive comments implying a slower-than-expected April, elongated sales cycles and smaller deals of late. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  sees an "element of ultra-conservatism" in the view, especially after a big miss last summer burned the company. NTAP off 15% at $27.96 premarket. (andrew.fitzgerald@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:46 (Dow Jones) While top-line durable goods orders come in slightly better than expected--up 0.2% in April--bookings for non-defense capital goods excluding aircraft decreased by 1.9%. That suggests businesses are wary about future demand and are reluctant to invest in more equipment. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-24-12 0919ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120524e85o0006j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
