\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Peru's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Used USD Repos For First Time\b0\par\par\uc2 1,189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2012\par 17:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:23 (Dow Jones) \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily so far this year to purchase US dollars and help ease appreciation pressures on the sol, yesterday used for the first time a new instrument designed to do just the opposite. With the sol under pressure, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $50M of one-day dollar-denominated repos, adding greenbacks to the market. So far no sign from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on whether they will repeat the move today. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightly controls volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, without setting a fixed level for the sol. (robert.kozak@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:19 (Dow Jones) The blue chips gathered steam ahead of the 11 o'clock hour, the annointed time for \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's trading debut. But so far, said debut is yet to happen, and, amid the anxiety, stocks have slid steadily as the clock ticks. Dow up 10. (michael.driscoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:18 (Dow Jones) The euro's rebound against the dollar gets extended to another high for the day as EU economics chief Olli Rehn says the commission is working on scenarios for Greece staying with the euro, which had been modestly higher already amid some short-covering. The euro traded as high as $1.2737 and is now at $1.2729, according to EBS via CQG. It traded at $1.2697 late Thursday. (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:13 (Dow Jones) \uc2 TIM Participacoes SA\uc2  (TSU, TIMP3.BR), the local unit of \uc2 Telecom Italia SpA\uc2  (TIT.MI), reached for the first time ever the leadership position in Brazil's largest city Sao Paulo, in terms mobile-phone market share. In April, TIM reached a market share of 31.30% in Sao Paulo, eclipsing \uc2 Telefonica Brasil SA\uc2  (VIV, VIVT4.BR) with a market share of 30.96% in the period. (rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:08 (Dow Jones) Remember those whispers of concern about how the NBA lockout could affect basketball gear sales? Well players eventually hit the courts in a shortened season, though it may not have mattered as new colorful shoes in the fashion-drive category helped drive demand. Still, added exposure couldn't hurt, and \uc2 Foot Locker\uc2  (FL) CEO \uc2 Ken Hicks\uc2  said key player shoes named after LeBron, Kobe and Durant sold "exceptionally well" in 1Q. He said color was a consistent theme in all athletic categories, a trend Hicks sees continuing. FL's strong F1Q report sends shares up 9.8% to $30.74, while rival Finish Line (FINL) jumped 7%. (john.kell@dowsjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 (Dow Jones) A bigger risk for ECB than losing money on Greek bonds in the event of a Greece euro-zone exit is a credibility crisis, says Jamie Stuttard, head of international bond portfolio management at \uc2 Fidelity Investments\uc2 . "While the ECB's balance sheet can withstand the losses, a key point is the risk of credibility as central banks buying bonds from countries that end up default," he says. With much at stake, the ECB may be prompted to act if the crisis deepens, he says, adding that it's important to check the volatility and velocity of the yield increase, especially in shorter-dated maturities in Spain and Italy, rather just focusing on the actual level of 10-year maturities. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:04 (Dow Jones) Bankrupt Jefferson County, Ala. plans to skip another G.O. debt payment in October, its attorney, Kenneth Klee, says. (The county defaulted on its non-bank owned G.O. debt for the first time last month.) The move is hardly surprising, given that state lawmakers killed a rescue bill for JeffCo this week. Alabama local governments have very limited powers and cannot raise revenue without state permission, so without help, Jefferson County is likely to stay in bankruptcy quite a while and may miss even more debt payments. Klee says under Alabama Supreme Court precedent, the county should provide for the health and welfare of its citizens instead of paying debt holders. (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:01 (Dow Jones) Chile's GDP isn't likely to continue growing at its current robust pace, Capital Economics says. Chile posted a 5.6% 1Q GDP gain while analysts expected a 5.2% increase. The solid performance of the Chilean economy likely won't last as copper prices, already down 10% so far this month, are expected to continue falling as the global economic turmoil will further intensify, says Capital Economics. Chile produces a third of the world's copper and the metal represents 15% of the country's GDP. (graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:00 (Dow Jones) After another week of pummelling, Chesapeake (CHK) is climbing along with natural-gas futures. Natural-gas producers are generally higher today amid the broader stock market's modest advance after a string of losses. CHK climbs 6% and Quicksilver gains 3% while Nymex natural-gas futures are up about 2%. (angel.gonzalez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:00 (Dow Jones) Is the hype over \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's (FB) IPO warranted? FB is a wonderful wide-moat business, but a choppy growth trajectory will likely send shares lower at some point, offering investors an entry point at a much more attractive valuation than they'll get today, says Morningstar analyst Rick Summer. He sees FB and \uc2 Google\uc2  (GOOG) dominating the Internet ad market as FB finds better ways to monetize its massive user base. Its trove of data on users is its ace in the hole as it races to develop new revenue streams, Summer says. (daisy.maxey@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:56 (Dow Jones) About 45,000 orders have been entered by \uc2 TD Ameritrade\uc2  (AMTD) customers to trade \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB) shares as of 10:30am EDT Friday, according to \uc2 Steve Quirk\uc2 , senior vice president of trading for the firm. About half \uc2 TD Ameritrade\uc2 's clients were able to get in on the allocation, says Quirk, who sees the social media giant's debut as a rallying point for individual investors. FB shares not yet trading. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 10:51 (Dow Jones) Colombia Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry said today the government will force some banks to boost reserve requirements on consumer credits as past-due loans begin to rise. Rules will only apply to banks with particularly high levels of overdue loans. Bancolombia (CIB), the country's top bank, said in its 1Q earnings report last week it's seeing a rise in delinquent consumer loans, and said it would thus focus more on corporate loans. Bancolombia's share price unchanged in Bogota at COP27,580, but down 6% since it noted the delinquencies. In NY, CIB up a fraction at $62.14. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.co \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-18-12 1123ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120518e85i000by\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
