\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: \uc2 BBVA\uc2  Says Peru's Sol Will Continue To Strengthen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2012\par 17:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:08 (Dow Jones) \uc2 BBVA\uc2  Continental expects the strengthening tendency of Peru's sol against the US dollar to continue in coming months and should close this year at about PEN2.60 per dollar. Yesterday, the sol closed at PEN2.652 per dollar, which is an appreciation of 1.7% so far this year. The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by buying dollars to ease volatility. "In this context, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to be active in the exchange market, moderating the pace that the PEN will strengthen," \uc2 BBVA\uc2  says. (ryan.dube@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:07 (Dow Jones) Ahead of JCPenney's (JCP) F1Q report Tuesday it's anyone's guess how the retailer will do--literally. Of the analysts providing estimates to Thomson Reuters, their bottom-line views range from a 51c loss to a 5c/share profit, a much wider range than has been typical for the company or peers. But JCP hasn't given insight on how it thought the quarter might go, leaving analysts on their own to figure out how JCP's pricing changes have impacted results here in the early going of the shift. Amid that uncertainty, shares have fallen 2.8% this year, trailing rivals \uc2 Kohl's\uc2  (KSS) and \uc2 Macy's\uc2  (M). (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:04 (Dow Jones) \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's (JPM) credit derivatives-trading losses are punishing finanical stocks, the only sector in the red, as the broader market pushes higher. JPM falls 8.2%, Citi (C) off 3%, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  (MS) loses 4.5%, Goldman (GS) declines 3% and BofA (BAC) gives back 0.5%. \uc2 The Financial Select Sector SPDR Fund\uc2  (XLF) is down 0.7%. "This just clouds the whole sector for a long time," says Rick Fier at Conifer Securities. (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:03 (Dow Jones) Tucked within the Univ of Michigan prelim May sentiment report are questions about how consumers view the housing market. Economists at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  report the average of the readings on home buying and selling conditions are up five of the last six months -- the best stretch without a decline in 15 years. "This move points to higher home sales in coming months," they conclude. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:57 (Dow Jones) Mexico's competitiveness in manufacturing keeps getting stronger, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says, noting that the country's market share for its principal export destination, the US, is near historic highs even as Mexico gradually diversifies into new markets. (US takes about 78% of Mexican exports, down from 90% a decade ago) An appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rapid wage gains in China have benefited Mexican manufacturers. "The greatest threat to the Mexican manufacturing model - China - is likely behind us," the brokerage says. (amy.guthrie@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:51 (Dow Jones) \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (JPM) may have suffered trading losses, but investment manager RegentAtlantic Capital thinks the bank is still in decent shape. "The loss in dollar terms is certainly significant, but the impact on their business we don't expect to be as significant," says its Director of Portfolio Management Greg Allison. Adds Tier 1 ratio still stronger than peers. But says announcement does raise questions over the bank's risk management. "Prior to the announcement, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  was ahead of its competition in risk control and that's the reason why it came out of 2008 in relatively good shape. The news does raise questions about whether this is a fluke or there's indication they don't have enough risk management." JPM down 8% at $37.45. (amy.or@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:49 (Dow Jones) General opinion on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's (JPM) trading fiasco seems to concur that perhaps the biggest hit comes to the bank's reputation and trust in its management, something that could eventually prove more costly than a $2B trading loss. "Dimon consistently misrepresented the seriousness of the exposures as soon as the press was onto it," Yves Smith writes at naked capitalism. "Both Bloomberg and the Wall Street Journal were digging, and Dimon was dismissive, calling the concerns a 'tempest in a teapot'." She says Dimon "misled investors, but no one seems to care much about niceties like accurate and timely disclosure these days." JPM down 8.2% at $37.39. (john.shipman@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.nakedcapitalism.com/2012/05/jamie-dimon-misrepresented-london-whale-risks-admits-to-2-billion-loss-plus-risk-management-black-eye.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.nakedcapitalism.com/2012/05/jamie-dimon-misrepresented-london-whale-risks-admits-to-2-billion-loss-plus-risk-management-black-eye.html}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 10:45 (Dow Jones) US beef exports slid from a year ago in March as sales to Mexico, South Korea and Japan weakened, according to USDA data compiled by the US Meat Export Federation. Sales for 1Q to the trio was down as well, with South Korea tumbling 56% in March and 36% for the quarter. Multiyear-to-record wholesale-beef prices hit in late February likely slowed buying interest, analysts say. (curt.thacker@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:40 (Dow Jones) Short interest for home builders rose in April. While many investors hoped the hard-hit sector had finally struck bottom, contrarian investors are clearly betting some of the stocks have further room to fall. According to \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , short interest rose 69 bps to 14.3%, led by the smaller-cap players, while the bigger names saw a modest overall rise. Short interest rose the most in Beazer (BZH)--up 955 bps--while Toll (TOL) saw a 195 bps climb. Short interest fell in some of the more troubled names: Hovnanian (HOV) fell 441 bps, while \uc2 KB Home\uc2  (KBH) dipped 268 bps. MDC slipped 63 bps. (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:40 (Dow Jones) For the commercial-paper market, for now the potential widespread downgrades of banks by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  is a bigger worry that \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's trading losses, says a commercial-paper trader at a primary dealer. "The JPM issue has the market a little bit worried because with controls in place, you don't expect such things to happen." CP trading is already slow as investors are concerned about the potential impact if \uc2 Moody's\uc2  does downgrade more than 100 banks, as well as the turmoil in Europe and proposed changes to the way money-market funds operate. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:38 (Dow Jones) Hovnanian (HOV) shares up 5.4% at $1.95 after \uc2 UBS\uc2  upgrades the struggling builder to buy from neutral, citing valuation. Depressed by liquidity concerns, HOV tumbled 23% since Jan 18, while peer group climbed 16%. But \uc2 UBS\uc2  notes that HOV is "generating greater operating leverage, through opening new, better located communities." The company is also tackling its financial situation, in part, with a recent equity raise. "While these transactions aren't a panacea, they represent a step in the right direction." (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:33 (Dow Jones) Investors were piling into derivatives, to bet, of course, on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's (JPM) derivatives bet gone awry. Options activity on the bank's shares surged to more than three-times the daily average volume within the first hour of trading as traders picked up options to protect against additional declines. One trader picked up 20,000 June $35 put options at a cost of $1.9M -- a bet that can hedge two-million JPM shares against an additional 6.5% drop. Shares down 8% at $37.48. (kaitlyn.kiernan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.co \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-11-12 1108ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120511e85b000cx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
