\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2 's Sol Appreciates But Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Limits Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 23:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sol appreciates but \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits gains\par \par \uc2 --Economists expect the strength of the sol to continue\par \par \uc2 --Sol appreciation less than in many other Latin American nations\par \par \uc2 By Robert Kozak\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 LIMA (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--Peru's sol has appreciated steadily in recent years and that appreciation has continued so far this year, although the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  has kept a lid on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect the sol's appreciation trend to continue, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase U.S. dollars, leaning against the wind for the nominally free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jefferies & Co\uc2  . Managing Director Siobhan Morden said in an email that she expects relative stability for the Peruvian sol, reflecting "credit strength and a heavily managed \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate."\par \par \uc2 That makes Peru's sol-denominated bonds more attractive.\par \par \uc2 "On the short list of defensive, low-beta exposure, the Peru Global PEN bonds consistently reaffirm its relative safe-haven status as the lowest beta \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and positive technicals with the onshore investors providing anchor support and valuation the same onshore/offshore," Morden said in a report.\par \par \uc2 The sol appreciated 4% last year and about 14% against the U.S. dollar since the end of 2008.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have capped the appreciation of the sol so far this year at 0.9% as of the end of trade on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 That is much less than in other Latin American nations, such as Brazil, where the real has appreciated about 6.2% this year against the U.S. dollar, while in Colombia the peso has gained some 8.9%. The Mexican peso is up about 7.2% against the U.S. dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate the flows in the sol," said Jose Razuri, an economist with Peruvian consulting firm, Maximixe.\par \par \uc2 The sol has been supported by strong inflows of foreign direct investments, which \uc2 Moody's\uc2  recently noted grew 4.5% in 2011.\par \par \uc2 "The increase is credit positive and came despite the uncertainty that surrounded the election of President Ollanta Humala and the deterioration of the global economy," it said.\par \par \uc2 Economists noted that Peruvian exporters in the past have complained long and loud when sol appreciation increases too rapidly as many have costs in soles but revenues in dollars.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials for their part have said repeatedly that they are concerned about mismatches in domestic loans, between those who have borrowed in soles but who have incomes in dollars.\par \par \uc2 The ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  meanwhile have driven up \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net international reserves, to $54 billion as of the end of February.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, to the end of February, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase $3.6 billion, more than in all of 2011. That compares with purchases of $9.0 billion in 2010.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has meanwhile left its reference interest rate unchanged at 4.25% since May last year.\par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires, 51 99927 7269, peru@dowjones.com [ 03-07-12 1743ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120307e837000zt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
